      "difficulty": "medium",
      "topic": "Influenza: antigenic shift",
      "stem": "A new influenza A strain spreads worldwide, causing a pandemic. Genetic analysis shows it has a hemagglutinin gene from an avian strain and other genes from human and swine strains. Which process produced it?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Reassortment of genome segments in a co-infected host",
        "B": "Transposition of a viral oncogene",
        "C": "Phenotypic mixing with no genetic change",
        "D": "Point mutations accumulating in hemagglutinin over time",
        "E": "Recombination between two DNA viruses"
      },
      "answer": "A",
      "explanation": "Influenza is an enveloped, segmented (8 segments), negative-sense ssRNA orthomyxovirus. Antigenic shift is reassortment of whole segments (HA or NA) between animal and human strains in a co-infected host, often pigs. It produces a new subtype and can cause pandemics. Antigenic drift is gradual point mutations in HA and NA, causing seasonal epidemics, which is why the vaccine is updated every year. Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid for entry, and neuraminidase releases new virions.",
      "wrong": {
        "D": "That is antigenic drift, which causes seasonal epidemics rather than pandemics.",
        "E": "Influenza is an RNA virus.",
        "C": "Phenotypic mixing is not heritable and cannot create a new strain.",
        "B": "Influenza carries no oncogene."
      },
      "figures": [
        {
          "file": "fig-flu-shift.webp",
          "caption": "Antigenic shift by reassortment: a cell co-infected with an avian strain and a human strain packages a mixture of the eight genome segments, producing a new strain with avian surface proteins and human-adapted internal genes.",
          "credit": {
            "artist": "Dhorspool at en.wikipedia",
            "lic": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
            "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Influenza_geneticshift.jpg",
            "title": "File:Influenza geneticshift.jpg"
          }
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        "Influenza A has a segmented genome of eight separate RNA pieces.",
        "If two different strains infect the same cell, usually in pigs or birds, the segments mix freely during packaging.",
        "A new virus can emerge carrying a hemagglutinin the human population has never seen.",
        "Because there is no pre-existing immunity, the new subtype can spread worldwide as a pandemic.",
        "Antigenic drift is the slower alternative: point mutations accumulate in HA and NA, causing seasonal epidemics and requiring yearly vaccine updates.",
        "Only segmented viruses can reassort, which is why shift happens with influenza but not with measles or rabies."
      ]
